course: medicine
lesson: 75
title: Dares: cliffs, roofs, tombstoning, railways and saying no
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: At the harbour Josh's mates are jumping off the end of the pier, «Don't be nesh, everyone's doing it!» («nesh» means soft); that's tombstoning, and the water's depth changes with the tide, with rocks hidden just under the surface, so jumpers break their backs and necks: some die, some never walk again.
explain: The same trap waits on cliff edges that crumble without warning, on old roofs with skylights that look solid and aren't, and on railways, where quiet fast trains need about a mile to stop, the third rail in parts of the south is always live, and overhead wires carry about 25,000 volts that can jump to you without a touch.
explain: Dares work because the brain wants to fit in with the group, which makes risky things feel normal, and online videos show the one try that worked, never the people who got hurt; a real mate never asks you to risk your life to prove anything.
explain: Saying no is easy to learn: Sophie, dared to lie in the road, grins «Nah, not for me, I'm off for chips, who's coming?», a quick no, a reason, and walking away, and often others follow; and if a friend plans something deadly, telling an adult is not snitching, it can save a life.
explain: This closes the safety block, and its rules all fit in one breath: swim between the flags and never go in after anyone (call, tell, throw), never go onto frozen ponds, helmet buckled and ears free on the bike, one at a time on trampolines and in the bowl, and no dare is worth your body.
fact: Tombstoning means jumping from piers, cliffs or bridges into water; tides change the depth, and hidden rocks or rubbish lie under the surface.
fact: Hitting rock, or even hitting water from high up, can break the back or neck; tombstoning has killed and paralysed people in the UK.
fact: Cliff edges crumble, especially after rain, and can be hollow underneath, so you keep well back, even for photos.
fact: Old roofs often have skylights and thin panels that break when stepped on, and a fall onto concrete can kill.
fact: Modern trains are quiet and very fast and can't swerve; at about 100 mph a train needs about a mile to stop.
fact: Railways are only crossed by bridges, underpasses or proper level crossings.
fact: Overhead railway wires carry about 25,000 volts, and the electricity can jump to you if you just get close.
fact: Many lines in the south of England have a third rail carrying about 750 volts, which is always live (full of electricity), train or no train.
fact: In a group, the wish to fit in makes risky things feel normal; this is peer pressure.
fact: A short, cheerful no with a reason, then walking away, works better than a long argument, and others often follow the one who says no; saying no to a group takes more guts than following it.
fact: Telling an adult about a friend's dangerous plan can save a life, and it isn't snitching or grassing (telling tales).
fact: Online challenge videos show the tries that worked, not the people who were hurt, and some challenges have killed children.
fact: «Bruv» is London slang for mate; «sus» is young slang for suspicious; «chicken» as an insult means scared.
exercise: What is tombstoning? | Jumping off piers, cliffs or bridges into water | Climbing sea cliffs with ropes | Diving at a proper swimming pool | Throwing stones at the sea
exercise: Why can a pier jump that was fine yesterday be deadly today? | The tide has changed the depth | The water gets softer overnight | Piers move about in the night | Sea water gets saltier overnight
exercise: What can hitting rock or water from high up do? | Break your back or neck | Just give you a red belly | Make you a better swimmer | Nothing if you keep your eyes shut
exercise: Amara steps back to the cliff edge for a selfie. What's the rule? | Keep well back from cliff edges | Step back only if the light is good | Stand on the edge if it looks solid | Only take selfies near cliffs in summer
exercise: Callum is dared to climb an old factory roof. What's the danger? | Skylights and thin panels break and he falls | The pigeons will peck him | The roof is too warm to stand on | Nothing, roofs hold pigeons so they hold kids
exercise: Riley crosses the tracks: «I'll hear the train». Why is that wrong? | Trains are quiet, fast and can't stop quickly | Trains always hoot for a whole minute | Trains stop when they see people | Rails are only dangerous at night
exercise: How far can a train at about 100 mph need to stop? | About a mile | About a metre | About a car length | About a football's width
exercise: Where should you cross a railway? | Bridges, underpasses or proper level crossings | Anywhere the track looks empty | Between two trains in a station | Wherever the fence is broken
exercise: Mo's friend wants to climb a wagon under the overhead wires. Why is that deadly? | 25,000 volts can jump to him without a touch | The wires are only dangerous if you touch them | The wagon could roll away slowly | The wires are switched off when parked
exercise: Jade wants to step across the tracks for her ball. What's true about the third rail? | It's live all the time and can kill | It's only live when a train is coming close | It's made of rubber, so it's safe | It's only live in the summer
exercise: Kabir's mates call him «chicken». What makes risky dares feel normal? | The brain's wish to fit in with the group | Bridges looking lower than they are | Rivers being warmer in groups | Mates knowing it's safe
exercise: Sophie is dared to lie in the road. What does she do right? | A quick no, a reason, and walks away | A long argument with them for half an hour | Lies down for five seconds only | Dares them to do it first
exercise: Ellie knows her friend plans to jump off the railway bridge. What should she do? | Tell an adult now | Keep quiet so she isn't a snitch | Go along and watch | Dare her to jump higher
exercise: Tyrone sees a dare challenge with a million views. What do views show? | What's popular, not what's safe | That it has been properly safety-tested | That nobody ever got hurt doing it | That it's legal everywhere in the world
exercise: A home socket is about 230 volts. Overhead wires carry about 25,000. How many times more? | Over 100 times | About 2 times | About 10 times | The same
exercise: When one of 5 mates says no, often 2 more join him. How many walk away safe? | 3 | 2 | 5 | 1
test: «Don't be nesh, someone jumped here yesterday». Why doesn't yesterday count? | The tide has changed and so has the depth | Yesterday the water was softer and kinder to land in | Piers grow taller overnight | Jumpers get luckier each day
test: Water is 6 m deep at high tide and drops by 5 m as the tide goes out. How much is left? | 1 m | 11 m | 5 m | 6 m
test: Kabir's mates: «bruv, you're so sus, just do it». What's actually true about saying no? | It takes more guts to say no than to follow | Saying no proves you're weak | You have to jump to stay mates | Saying no is only for little kids and babies
test: Jade: «It's only one rail, innit, just don't touch it». What's the right move? | Leave the ball and tell staff or an adult | Step over the rail very carefully | Wait for a gap between trains | Hook the ball out with a stick
test: In an action film the hero runs along the train roof under the wires. What's real? | The wires' power can jump to you and kill | Train roofs are safe as long as you keep moving fast | Wires only hurt cartoon villains | Electricity can't reach a moving train
test: «If I tell, everyone will call me a snitch». What's worse than being called a snitch? | Losing a mate forever | Getting a detention | Missing the bus home | Having to explain it twice
test: «Go on, it's just a roof, you scared or what?» What would a real mate do? | Never dare you to risk your life | Dare you to go even higher | Film it for the group chat | Climb up first to show it's safe
test: «it's got a million views, no cap» (no cap = no lie). Imagine 100 tries filmed and only 1 shown. How many tries don't you see? | 99 | 100 | 1 | 101
test: What would happen if Sophie argued for ten minutes instead of «nah, I'm off for chips»? | The pressure would grow; a quick exit works better | They'd all agree with her at once | She'd win a prize for arguing | The dare would get easier to do
test: Amara: «the light's lush, one more step back». What can the edge do? | Give way under her without warning | Hold her better after a bit of rain | Warn her by creaking loudly before it goes | Shift slowly, so she has time to step away
test: Riley: «takes two secs». Which rule fits every safety lesson in this block? | No shortcut or dare is worth your body | Quick risks are always fine | Only grown-ups need safety rules, not kids | If you're fast, nothing can happen
test: Pick the rule that sums up water safety from this block. | Never go in after anyone: call, tell, throw | Jump in fast to save a friend | Swim out past the flags to reach the big waves | Walk out on ice to fetch a dog
exam: Identify the principal hazards associated with the practice known as tombstoning. | Variable depth owing to tides and submerged rocks | The excessive warmth of coastal waters | The prohibition of photography at harbours | The risk of mild sunburn alone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the third rail upon certain railway lines in the south of England? | It is energised at all times and may cause death | It is energised only upon the approach of a train | It is constructed of non-conducting rubber | It is energised only during the summer months
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning overhead railway power lines. | Electricity may pass to a person in proximity without contact | Contact is required for any injury to occur | The lines are de-energised when wagons are stationary | The lines carry a voltage lower than a domestic socket
exam: It is given that a pupil is aware of a peer's intention to jump from a railway bridge. State the appropriate course of action. | An adult is to be informed without delay | The matter is to be kept strictly confidential | The pupil is to accompany the peer | The pupil is to await the outcome
